    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> kingdom, formed when the tribes broke from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>’s heir c. 930 BCE, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>.</li><li>Larger and richer than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, it fielded 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Qarqar</a> in 853 BCE under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> in 722 BCE and deported its people, giving rise to the legend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-lost-tribes">Ten Lost Tribes</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Assyrian scribes went on calling Israel ‘the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">House of Omri</a>’ for well over a century, long after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> had wiped out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>’s dynasty.</p></aside>
    </section>

