    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomon.jpg" alt="Solomon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simeon_Solomon,_King_Solomon,_1872_or_1874,_NGA_76152_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Simeon Solomon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Israel, often called the Northern Kingdom, arose c. 930 BCE when the northern tribes broke away from the United Kingdom of Israel after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. According to the Books of Kings, an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> asked Solomon’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rehoboam">Rehoboam</a> to lighten the forced labour and taxes of his father’s reign; when he refused, the northerners made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam I</a> their king, a former overseer of Solomon’s labour gangs who had taken refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shishak</a>. The extent and character of the earlier united monarchy are debated by historians and archaeologists, but the existence of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdoms, Israel in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the south, is secure. Jeroboam ruled first from Shechem and later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirzah">Tirzah</a>, and he set up royal sanctuaries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a> and Dan, each with a golden calf, so that his subjects need not go on pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a>. The first decades brought border wars with Judah and a succession of coups. Stability came only with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, an army commander who seized power c. 885 BCE and founded a new capital on the hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, which he bought, according to the Bible, from a man named Shemer.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samaria.jpg" alt="Samaria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Views_from_Sebastia_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daniel Ventura, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Israel held the more fertile and populous part of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>: the hill country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezreel-valley">Jezreel Valley</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a> east of the Jordan. Most people were farmers and herders living in villages, organised in clans and households that held land by inheritance. The staple products were grain, wine and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, pressed in large installations. The Samaria ostraca, inked potsherds of the eighth century BCE, record deliveries of wine and oil to the capital from the surrounding estates. Close ties with the Phoenician cities, sealed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>’s marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a>, daughter of the king of Sidon, brought luxury goods and craftsmen, and the carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> panels found at Samaria show the court’s taste for Phoenician work. Religion centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>, worshipped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a>, Dan and many local shrines, but other gods were honoured beside him: inscriptions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuntillet-ajrud">Kuntillet Ajrud</a> in Sinai invoke ‘Yahweh of Samaria and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>’, and the Bible describes a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> in Samaria. In the prosperous eighth century wealth gathered in the hands of the court and great landowners, and the prophets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amos-prophet">Amos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a> denounced the luxury of Samaria, the oppression of the poor and the worship of other gods.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar.jpg" alt="Battle of Qarqar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Battle of Qarqar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karkar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yuber, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the ninth century BCE Israel was the strongest military power among the small kingdoms of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Its army combined a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> corps and professional troops with levies from the tribes and towns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">Omride</a> kings built or rebuilt fortified centres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezreel-city">Jezreel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> and Hazor, with casemate walls, multi-chambered gates and large royal enclosures; the pillared buildings at Megiddo once credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> are now often dated to this period, although whether they were stables is still debated. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Battle of Qarqar</a> in 853 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> joined a coalition of Levantine rulers against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurkh-monoliths">Kurkh Monolith</a> credits ‘Ahab the Israelite’ with 2,000 chariots and 10,000 foot soldiers, the largest chariot contingent in the coalition. Israel fought Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> for decades over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a>, and according to the Bible a king of Israel, traditionally Ahab, fell in battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramoth-gilead">Ramoth-gilead</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a> and his son held Moab in subjection until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha">King Mesha</a> rebelled. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael of Damascus</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Arameans</a> took much of Israel’s territory in the later ninth century, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoash-of-israel">Joash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-ii">Jeroboam II</a> won it back. Against the standing armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> and his successors, however, Israel had no effective answer.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i.jpg" alt="Jeroboam I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Honor%C3%A9_Fragonard_-_Jeroboam_Offering_Sacrifice_for_the_Idol_-_WGA08049_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean-Honoré Fragonard, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king of Israel was anointed and claimed divine sanction, but the northern monarchy never created a lasting royal line. Of the nineteen or so kings named in the Bible, several were murdered, and the crown passed through a series of short-lived houses: those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baasha">Baasha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>, followed by a string of usurpers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">House of Omri</a> was the most influential, and Assyrian scribes long called Israel Bit Humri, the ‘House of Omri’, even after his line had fallen. Jehu, an army commander, overthrew it c. 841 BCE in a coup supported by the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisha">Elisha</a>, killing King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Joram of Israel</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaziah-of-judah">Ahaziah of Judah</a>, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Tel Dan</a> Stele, probably set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael of Damascus</a>, claims those deaths for the Aramean king. Jehu’s dynasty lasted almost a century, the longest in Israel’s history. Royal government rested on court officials, district governors and fortified towns, and on produce collected from crown estates, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> ostraca show. Prophets formed an independent voice that could challenge kings: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a>’s promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a> condemned the court’s shifting alliances. The army was the decisive power broker, and most changes of dynasty were military coups, whereas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a> on its throne throughout.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesha-stele.jpg" alt="Mesha Stele" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1120870_Louvre_st%C3%A8le_de_M%C3%A9sha_AO5066_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Israel was the first of the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> kingdoms to appear in foreign records. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a> names ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, king of Israel’, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurkh-monoliths">Kurkh Monolith</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-obelisk-of-shalmaneser-iii">Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III</a> shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>, or his envoy, prostrate before the Assyrian king. Its greater legacy lay in the traditions it handed on. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, refugees from the north probably settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> grew sharply in the late eighth century, and northern texts and memories were absorbed into what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>. The prophecies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amos-prophet">Amos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a>, the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisha">Elisha</a>, and the Books of Kings’ verdict on the ‘sins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam</a>’ all concern the northern kingdom, although they reach us through Judahite editors who judged it harshly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>, who survive as a small community at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holon">Holon</a>, trace their descent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> who remained in the land. The deportees became the subject of the legend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-lost-tribes">Ten Lost Tribes</a>, which inspired speculation about their whereabouts from Antiquity to the modern era. Archaeologically, Samaria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a>, Hazor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezreel-city">Jezreel</a> and Dan remain among the most intensively studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> sites of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii.jpg" alt="Tiglath-pileser III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tilglath_pileser_iii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Israel’s last decades were shaped by the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> (r. 745–727 BCE). After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-ii">Jeroboam II</a> the throne changed hands repeatedly by murder: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zechariah-of-israel">Zechariah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shallum-of-israel">Shallum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menahem">Menahem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekahiah">Pekahiah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah</a> all reigned within about fifteen years. Menahem paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> a tribute of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, raised by a levy on wealthy landowners. Pekah instead joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin of Damascus</a> in an anti-Assyrian alliance and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> when its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> refused to take part, the conflict known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syro-ephraimite-war">Syro-Ephraimite War</a>. Ahaz appealed to Assyria, and in 733–732 BCE Tiglath-pileser III took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, the coastal plain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a>, and deported part of their population, leaving Israel a rump state around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshea">Hoshea</a>, who killed Pekah and became an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, later stopped paying tribute and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-v">Shalmaneser V</a> besieged Samaria, which fell in 722 BCE after a siege the Bible puts at three years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> claimed the conquest, crushed a further revolt in 720 BCE, deported some 27,000 inhabitants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-region">Media</a>, settled people from other lands in their place, and made Samaria the centre of the Assyrian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samerina">Samerina</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Israel was the larger and wealthier of the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> kingdoms, a regional military power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>, and the setting of the prophets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amos-prophet">Amos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a>; its destruction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> in 722 BCE shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and the legend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-lost-tribes">Ten Lost Tribes</a>.</em></p>

